# Nothing in this repository redistributes a game that is still under copyright.
#
# Three kinds of file are therefore never committed:
#
#   original/    the game exactly as it shipped
#   recovered/   a reconstruction that assembles or compiles to a byte-identical
#                copy of it — legally the same thing as the binary, just in
#                source form, and easy to forget for exactly that reason
#   reference/   anything *derived* from the game: extracted sprites, memory
#                dumps, screenshots, disk images. A sprite sheet pulled out of
#                a copyrighted game is still that game, and this is the rule
#                people forget, because a PNG does not feel like a binary
#
# Every game folder follows this convention, so the rule is one pattern each.
# Everything else — the documentation, the port, our own tools — is our own work
# and is committed normally.
#
# Each game's README explains how to regenerate what is missing from a copy of
# the game you already own.

*/original/
*/recovered/
*/reference/

# Exception: spyder/reference/ is not derived from the game. It is our own
# playable art-direction reference scene (procedural code, bundled OFL fonts,
# our own screenshots), so it is committed like the rest of the port.
!spyder/reference/
spyder/reference/tools/node_modules/
spyder/.scratch/

# ancient-art-of-war: decompiled with the DOS-Decompiler toolkit's
# DECOMPILE.md/SPECIFY.md prompts, which name their outputs src/ and spec/
# instead of recovered/ and reference/ above. Both hold material derived
# from the game (Ghidra's C, decoded campaign and opponent tables, terrain
# masks) and fall under the same rule; see ancient-art-of-war/CLAUDE.md.
ancient-art-of-war/src/
ancient-art-of-war/spec/

# karateka: same case. src/ is the DECOMPILE.md run (Ghidra's C, decoded
# sprites/backdrops, CHECKLIST.md/REPORT.md); review/ is a read-only audit of
# it; spec/ is the SPECIFY.md output (game-spec, rule modules, decoded
# tables). All three hold material derived from the game; see
# karateka/CLAUDE.md.
karateka/src/
karateka/review/
karateka/spec/

# spyder: before this folder became the original house-cleaning
# reimagining (DESIGN.md, STORY.md, game/, reference/), it was a
# DOS-Decompiler target for the real 1988 shareware game SPYDER.EXE.
# src/ and spec/ are that run's output — decompiled.c, symbols.json,
# and PNGs decoded straight from the game's BMAPS.PIC — so they hold
# the same derived material as ancient-art-of-war/karateka above, and
# original/ (the game's own files) is already caught by */original/.
spyder/src/
spyder/spec/

# Belt and braces: never commit a game binary, an archive of one, or a disk
# image, from anywhere in the tree.
*.com
*.exe
*.COM
*.EXE
*.zip
*.dsk
*.DSK
*.woz
*.po
*.do
*.img

# Editor and OS noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*.swp
*~
.vscode/
.idea/

# Python bytecode
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Scratch space for unpacked images, Ghidra projects, emulator dumps.
# Nothing here is worth committing, and some of it is the game.
*/work/

# Hening: anything that contains the original rooms stays local
sokoban/game/src/data/*.generated.json
sokoban/game/src/data/solutions.manual.json
sokoban/game/dist/
sokoban/game/node_modules/
sokoban/review/
sokoban/.scratch/
# Hening: also local — the decompile runs of the 1984 game (src/, src-game/:
# its level files and screenshots), a copy of reference/index.html (which
# embeds three original rooms), crew run logs, and tool caches.
sokoban/src/
sokoban/src-game/
sokoban/index.html
sokoban/node_modules/
sokoban/game/.scratch/
sokoban/prompts/logs/

